.TH BLUE "1" "August 2026" "blue v0.3.7-1ab4f0e-darwin/arm64" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blue \- blue - a fun programming language
.SH DESCRIPTION
blue is a tool for running blue source code
.SS "Usage:"
.IP
blue <command> [arguments]
.PP
The commands are:
.TP
lex
start the lexer repl or lex the given file (converts the file to tokens and prints)
.TP
parse
start the parser repl or parse the given file (converts the file to an inspectable AST without node names)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-parser\-errors\fR
show all parser errors instead of stopping at the first one
.TP
doc
print the help strings of all publicly accesible functions in the given filepath or module
.IP
note: the file/module will be compiled to gather all functions
.TP
vm
run the given string or file through the VM (a .bluec binary image is run directly without recompiling)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-parser\-errors\fR
show all parser errors instead of stopping at the first one
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-exec\fR
do not allow executing programs or scripts
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, e, eval
alternative ways to trigger the vm evaluation
.TP
compile
compiles the given string or file to bytecode
.TP
\fB\-o\fR <file>
write a compiled .bluec binary image instead of printing bytecode.
run it with: blue vm out.bluec, bluerun out.bluec, or bundle it into an executable
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-tokens\fR
strip the token table from the image (smaller file, error traces lose file/line info)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-parser\-errors\fR
show all parser errors instead of stopping at the first one
.TP
bundle
compile the given .b file and append it to a copy of the minimal bluerun runner template,
producing a single self\-contained executable. the template is looked up next to this
executable as bluerun\-<GOOS>\-<GOARCH> (or bluerun); pass \fB\-\-go\-build\fR to build it with go instead
.TP
\fB\-o\fR <file>
path of the bundled executable to write
.TP
\fB\-\-go\-build\fR
build the template on the fly using the local go toolchain
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-parser\-errors\fR
show all parser errors instead of stopping at the first one
.TP
help
prints this help message
.TP
version
prints the current version
.PP
The default behavior for no command/arguments will start an vm repl. (If given a file, the file will be evaluated with the vm)
.PP
A '\-' can be given in place of a file to read the program from STDIN. When no
command is given and stdin is piped or redirected, the piped program is evaluated:
.IP
echo 'println(1 + 2)' | blue
blue \- < program.b
cat program.b | blue vm \-
.PP
Errors are printed to STDERR so that program output and errors can be separated.
.PP
Environment Variables:
.PP
BLUE_DISABLE_HTTP_SERVER_DEBUG   set to true to disable the http server route path printing and message
.PP
BLUE_INSTALL_PATH                set to the path where the blue src is installed. ie. ~/.blue/src
.PP
NO_COLOR or BLUE_NO_COLOR        set to true (or any non empty string) to disable colored printing
.PP
PATH                             add blue to the path variable to access it anywhere. ie. ~/.blue/bin could be added to path with the blue exe inside of it
